{"step_index":52,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-18T04:52:53Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-18T12:52:53+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-18T12:52:53+08:00\nShowing lines 1-379 of 379\n1: ---\n2: name: swarm\n3: description: >-\n4:   (Preview & Quota Heavy) Run a swarm: several agents working in parallel on\n5:   one task, each in its own conversation, collaborating through a shared\n6:   message board. Use when the work splits into pieces that can be done at the\n7:   same time, or when a second pair of eyes should check the first pair's work.\n8: metadata:\n9:   icon: hub\n10:   logo: logo.svg\n11: ---\n12: \n13: # You are the swarm coordinator\n14: \n15: A swarm is a set of workers, each its own agent in its own conversation, that\n16: collaborate by posting to one shared message board. You are the coordinator:\n17: the user talks to you and to nobody else, and the workers talk to you and to\n18: each other on the board.\n19: \n20: Your hands are four tools:\n21: \n22: | Tool | What it is for |\n23: |---|---|\n24: | `swarm` | `status`, `models`, `add`, `model`, `kill`, `title` — the swarm itself |\n25: | `post_message` | say something on the board; this is how you assign work |\n26: | `read_board` | read what has been said |\n27: | `swarm_wait` | sleep until something is posted; costs nothing while asleep |\n28: \n29: ## Do not do the work\n30: \n31: Technical work goes to the swarm. A coordinator writing code is a coordinator\n32: not watching the swarm, and an unwatched swarm quietly stops. Reading is not\n33: doing: read what comes back, judge it, and decide what happens next — that part\n34: is the job.\n35: \n36: It is not just code. Investigations, research, builds/tests, syncs, uploads and\n37: review fixes also go to workers; answering the user from a quick read-only lookup is\n38: fine. Keep your own turns short so you stay free to respond to the user — brief,\n39: reply, and end the turn. Ending a turn already parks you, so a turn that answers\n40: the user needs no `swarm_wait`; call `swarm_wait` only to park with nothing to\n41: say. Never sit inside a long task of your own while the user waits. When the\n42: u\n<truncated 18823 bytes>\n - **The board outlives the turn.** Everything said is on it, in order, forever;\n356:   `read_board{since:0}` from the beginning if you lose your place.\n357: - **Channels only work if `main` stays quiet.** Area chatter in `main` wakes\n358:   every worker, exactly as if there were no channels. Do not over-split either:\n359:   a channel per worker is no channels at all. Archive an area when it is done.\n360: - **A worker (or you) can run out of quota.** If a worker's turn fails with an error\n361:   showing quota exhaustion (`status` still calls it `waiting`), or a member\n362:   receives a `[quota]` low-quota warning at turn start, switch it to\n363:   another model with `swarm{action:\"model\", name, model}`: it keeps its\n364:   conversation and picks up where it left off on the new model. Any member\n365:   (including you, with `name:\"coordinator\"` or `name` omitted, or a worker\n366:   naming itself) can also switch its own model with `swarm{action:\"model\", model:\"<model>\"}`.\n367:   `swarm{action:\"models\"}` shows live `quota_remaining` and `quota_resets` for\n368:   each model, and `add` and `model` refuse a model whose quota is exhausted\n369:   unless AI credits are paying for it (`models` then says so in `quota_note`).\n370:   When switching a worker, leave `model` off to use your own (since you are\n371:   still running, it is guaranteed not to be out of quota yet), or name the\n372:   model the user asked for. Do not `kill` and re-`add` it. Tell the user which\n373:   model ran out and what you switched to. Switching another worker only works\n374:   when it is `waiting`, so wait for one that is `working` to stop first.\n375: - **Nothing restarts on its own after quota runs out.** A member whose turn\n376:   failed on quota sits out until the board moves, its model is switched, or the\n377:   user messages it. When the user tells you to continue, switch each member\n378:   that ran out (the error messages you got name them), or, if quota is back or\n379:   AI credits now cover it, post to each by name so it picks up its work.","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":53,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-18T04:52:53Z","input_tokens":9253,"cache_read_tokens":236650,"output_tokens":6171,"thinking":"I'm splitting the work so each piece has its own files, since v5_env.py is the one most likely to collide. The env worker takes ownership of the scene builder and handles the origin bug, the fence posts, the masts, brake poles, merged truss beams, pit box lines, and the empty collection cleanup, then rebuilds env and anim and checks frames.\n\nI'm weighing how to divide the remaining work so the tidy and verifier steps wait on the env worker's results. The docs and archive cleanup should only happen once env has confirmed its output, and I'm considering whether the clay and color re-renders are worth their long run times given the clay video is broken.\n\nNext I'm finishing the brief for the env worker: it needs the Blender path, the bug locations, and the rule that the clay re-render only starts after the verifier passes, while I leave the color re-render for the user to decide.\n\nI'm settling the env worker's fix for the scene builder: refresh the view layer before reading world matrices, and only re-origin meshes whose location is zero. For assembly parenting, I'm weighing empties named under the existing V5_Stand_ prefix so they group with their stands, keeping children's transforms intact.\n\nI'm settling on merged per-stand meshes for the repetitive small parts, like the truss, columns, and flags with their poles, while keeping the larger pieces such as the tier blocks, back, roof, and VIP separate. The pit box lines would collapse into a single mesh, and I'm still working out how the brake board poles should be handled.\n\nI'm planning the verifier's role: it will work read-only, running the audit scripts from its own scratch folder, and I'm considering pointing it at a dedicated path under the brain directory.\n\nNext I'm sorting out the handoffs: the verifier checks the env worker's output using the existing audit scripts, and only after a pass does the env worker re-render the clay video and the tidy worker finish the docs and cleanup.\n\nI'm finishing the env worker's brief and deciding that it owns v5_anim.py too, so the anim edits don't collide with a rebuild. The tidy worker stays small, handling docs and file cleanup. Next I'll check the workspace setting, which should inherit, and then write the briefs.\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"swarm","args":{"action":"\"title\"","title":"\"Silverstone V5 scene cleanup\""}},{"name":"swarm","args":{"action":"\"add\"","brief":"\"You own the scene build for the Blender project at d:\\\\项目session\\\\blender-supercar: **pipeline/v5_env.py** and **pipeline/v5_anim.py**. Nobody else edits these two files. The user wants: remove redundant models, keep the scene very easy for a human to edit, and keep improving it. Read README.md and docs/README_V5.md first.\\n\\nBlender 5.3 (CPU only, EEVEE): `D:\\\\APPs\\\\steam\\\\steamapps\\\\common\\\\Blender\\\\blender.exe -b --factory-startup -P <script>`. Pipeline: v5_env.py (reads pipeline/v4_stage_rig.blend → writes LATEST/v5_stage_env.blend, about 3 min) → v5_anim.py (→ LATEST/v5_stage_anim.blend, about 1 min) → v5_clay.py (→ LATEST/v5_stage_anim_clay.blend). The shell is PowerShell and prints a big ASCII banner first, which you can ignore.\\n\\nProblems already confirmed (fix all of them):\\n1. **CRITICAL: 441 of 672 V5_ objects are piled at world (0,0,0)** in the current LATEST/v5_stage_env.blend, v5_stage_anim.blend and the clay file. The cause is step 13 of v5_env.py (around lines 1059-1072). It reads `ob.matrix_world` on objects that were just created and never got a depsgraph update, so every `box()`/sphere object (they set `ob.location`) has an identity matrix_world, and the code sets `ob.location = (0,0,0)`. That hits ad boards, stand columns/trusses/posts/back/VIP/roof strips, the gantry, bridges, screens, marshal posts, hangars, the pit garage, start lights and brake boards. The clay video renders_v5/f1_silverstone_clay_1080p.mp4 is broken because of this (renders_v5/clay_frames/f_0001.jpg is a wall of boxes). Fix it with `bpy.context.view_layer.update()` before reading matrix_world, and only re-origin objects whose mesh is baked in world space (location == 0).\\n2. `V5_FencePosts` (around lines 623-638): the `for dx, dy` loop ignores dx/dy, so all 972 faces have zero area and are invisible. Offset ±0.07 m along the track tangent so they are real thin posts.\\n3. `V5_FieldMasts` (around lines 1020-1037): the pole faces use absolute indices `(0,1,5,4)…` instead of `base+…`. The result is 44 dup\n<truncated 2518 bytes>","name":"\"env\""}},{"name":"swarm","args":{"action":"\"add\"","brief":"\"You are the verifier for the Blender project at d:\\\\项目session\\\\blender-supercar. Worker `env` is fixing pipeline/v5_env.py and v5_anim.py and rebuilding LATEST/v5_stage_env.blend, v5_stage_anim.blend and v5_stage_anim_clay.blend. You report problems; you do not edit project files. Write any scripts you need under C:\\\\Users\\\\86052\\\\.gemini\\\\antigravity\\\\brain\\\\16b5f3cb-4bce-43d1-a09a-ebf98bd665b7\\\\scratch\\\\ . Existing helpers there: audit_scene.py (heavy meshes, loose verts, bbox overlap groups, images) and check_origin_pile.py (counts V5_ meshes whose world bbox center is near (0,0,0)). Blender: `D:\\\\APPs\\\\steam\\\\steamapps\\\\common\\\\Blender\\\\blender.exe -b --factory-startup -P <script>` (PowerShell; ignore the ASCII banner).\\n\\nBaseline before the fix: 919 objects in env and 948 in anim; 441 of 672 V5_ meshes piled at world origin (the critical bug); V5_FencePosts all zero-area faces; V5_FieldMasts with duplicate faces and 88 loose verts; 18 duplicate brake-board pole objects; about 172 tiny 0.16 m truss cubes. LATEST/v5_stage_env.blend1 (dated 11/14) is an older build with correct placement; use it as a placement reference.\\n\\nWait until @env posts that the rebuild is ready, then check:\\n1. No V5_ objects are piled at the origin. Assemblies sit where they did in the 11/14 reference: compare world bbox centers of stands, ad boards, garage, bridges, screens, hangars.\\n2. Fence posts and field masts are real geometry; no duplicate co-located objects or duplicate faces; no loose verts in V5_ meshes.\\n3. Editability: each assembly has an Empty parent at its center, and moving it moves all its children (test by translating the Empty in a script and checking children world positions). Collections V5_01..V5_07 are intact. Origins of individual objects are at their geometry, not 1 km away. The empty collections 09_Studio_Stage_Rig and WheelSpokes_* are gone.\\n4. Car meshes, rig, 26 cameras and timeline markers, the 2161-frame range and materials are unchanged. The v5_anim physics self-check prints PASS.\\n5. Look at e\n<truncated 248 bytes>","name":"\"verifier\""}},{"name":"swarm","args":{"action":"\"add\"","brief":"\"You own project docs and file housekeeping for the Blender project at d:\\\\项目session\\\\blender-supercar: README.md, LATEST/README.txt, docs/README_V5.md, and moving or deleting redundant files. Do NOT edit pipeline/v5_env.py or pipeline/v5_anim.py (worker `env` owns them) and do not touch the .blend files while env is rebuilding.\\n\\nStart now (safe in parallel):\\n1. pipeline/v5_organize.py is superseded by step 13 of v5_env.py. Move it to archive/scripts/ (create a v5 subfolder) and remove references to it in README.md.\\n2. Fix stale facts in docs/README_V5.md: section 7 lists `pipeline/v5_stage_env.blend` / `pipeline/v5_stage_anim.blend`, but they live in LATEST/. Several places say \\\"25 机位\\\" but there are 26 cameras. Section 7 does not mention the clay files (v5_clay.py, render_clay.py, mux_clay.sh, LATEST/v5_stage_anim_clay.blend) or v5_viewport_fast.py. README.md line 9 says LATEST holds only two files, but it also holds the clay blend.\\n3. Draft (do not yet write the final numbers) a short \\\"编辑指南 / editing guide\\\